public class ProductModStack {
    private static final long MOD = 1_000_000_007;

    private static class Node {
        int value;
        long productMod;
        Node next;

        Node(int value, long productMod, Node next) {
            this.value = value;
            this.productMod = productMod;
            this.next = next;
        }
    }

    private Node top;

    public void push(int value) {
        long newProduct;
        if (top == null) {
            newProduct = value % MOD;
        } else {
            newProduct = (top.productMod * (long)value) % MOD;
        }
        top = new Node(value, newProduct, top);
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        int value = top.value;
        top = top.next;
        return value;
    }

    public long getProductMod() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return top.productMod;
    }
}
```

**Why this feature?** It's more interesting than min/max (which are standard), and it demonstrates the key insight: each node caches an aggregate computed from all elements below it, so adding a new element just requires one modular multiplication. All three operations are truly O(1).

